// prism_ui_rating -- rating and review display components (prism/ui/, milestone MB.14): a star
// rating readout, a review card, a rating-breakdown summary, a vertical review list, and a
// compact score badge. Every one is a COMPOSITION over the closed 22-primitive vocabulary
// (widget/), built through prism_content/prism_arrange directly and prism_ui_kit.nova wherever
// it fits -- no new PrismNodeKind (PRISM_SPEC.md axiom A7), the same discipline every
// prism_ui_*.nova file in this codebase follows. This is the Ant Design `Rate` family plus the
// review-breakdown block seen on every e-commerce product page (Amazon's "4.2 out of 5, 150
// ratings" bar chart, a product review feed).
//
// ── THIS IS NOT prism_ui_input.prism_in_rating, AND THE OVERLAP IS DELIBERATE, NOT MISSED ──────
// Grepped before writing a line of this file (CLAUDE.md's "does this already exist" discipline):
// `prism_ui_input.nova` (MB.4) already has `prism_in_rating(label, value, max_val)`, an
// EDITABLE-looking star control built the same "Check() for filled, Minus() for empty" way this
// file's own `_prism_rt_star_flow` builds its stars. That is NOT a coincidence, and it is NOT
// this file importing that logic -- ui/*.nova files never cross-import each other anywhere in
// this codebase (verified: every `import prism_ui_*` in prism/ui/*.nova names only
// `prism_ui_kit`, never a sibling), so the tiny check/minus loop is re-derived here exactly the
// way `prism_ui_stat.prism_st_direction_icon` re-derives `prism_ui_data`'s identical
// StatUp/StatDown/StatFlat icon mapping instead of importing it. What is genuinely DIFFERENT
// between the two files, not a re-skin of the same component:
//   - `prism_in_rating` renders `prism_ui_labeled(label, band(stars))` -- label ABOVE a control,
//     the FORM-FIELD layout convention every other prism_ui_input.nova component uses (date,
//     multiselect, search, stepper all stack label-then-control). It belongs to the INPUT family:
//     a rating someone is IN THE PROCESS OF SETTING.
//   - `prism_rt_stars` here renders `band[label, flow(icons)]` -- label INLINE beside the stars,
//     the READ-ONLY DISPLAY convention this file's task explicitly calls for and every review/
//     summary layout below actually needs (a review card's "Jane Doe  ★★★★☆" header is one line,
//     not a label stacked over a control). It belongs to the DISPLAY family: a rating someone
//     ALREADY GAVE, being shown back.
//   - Only `prism_rt_stars` and `prism_rt_review` need the star glyphs; `prism_rt_summary`,
//     `prism_rt_review_list`, and `prism_rt_score_badge` have no equivalent in prism_ui_input.nova
//     at all -- that file has no notion of a review, a rating breakdown, or a compact score.
// `_prism_rt_star_flow` is `flow`, not `band` -- see the next note -- so it is not even
// byte-identical output to `prism_in_rating`'s row despite sharing the same icon vocabulary.
//
// ── WHY THE STAR ROW IS A `flow`, NOT prism_in_rating's `band` ──────────────────────────────────
// PRISM_SPEC.md §7: `flow` is "inline children that wrap", `band` is fixed inline axis with no
// wrap. `prism_in_rating` is a small, bounded control (a form field, realistically 5-10 stars)
// where wrapping is never a concern. `prism_rt_stars`/`prism_rt_review` have no such bound on
// `rt_max`/`rt_max_rating` (only `>= 1`) -- a caller building a "score out of 100" bar (see
// `prism_rt_score_badge`'s own "85/100" example) using this same star primitive would produce
// 100 icons, which MUST wrap at narrow widths or it silently overflows the viewport with no
// layout recourse (same reasoning `prism_st_dashboard`'s header already gives for choosing `flow`
// over `band` for its own unbounded-width row of cards).
//
// ── WHY rt_average IS AN int (TENTHS), NEVER A float ────────────────────────────────────────────
// "4.2 average" needs a fractional digit, but this codebase has two separate documented float
// traps (reference_nova_float_codegen_geo_bearing, reference_nova_float_int_compare_unsound) and
// `prism_fb_progress` (prism_ui_feedback.nova) already established the working alternative for
// exactly this shape of problem: represent the fraction as a scaled integer and split it with `/`
// and `%`, never a float. `rt_average` is therefore the average rating in TENTHS -- 42 means
// "4.2" -- decoded as `rt_average / 10` and `rt_average % 10`, both integer operations. No float
// appears anywhere in this file, matching that file's own stated invariant.
//
// ── WHY THE DISTRIBUTION BAR IS PROPORTIONAL TO rt_count, NOT TO THE LARGEST BUCKET ─────────────
// Two normalizations were considered: relative to the largest single bucket (a plain histogram,
// "the tallest bar is always full"), or relative to `rt_count` (the realistic Amazon/e-commerce
// convention, "the 5-star bar's length IS the fraction of all reviews that gave 5 stars"). The
// second was chosen because `rt_count` is a caller-supplied, semantically meaningful total ("N
// reviews", not a derived artifact of the sample), and it matches this exact "N stars, M reviews"
// framing everywhere it's actually used in the real world -- a 3-star bucket with only 2 of 150
// reviews should read as nearly empty, not proportionally scaled up just because it happens to be
// the biggest of five sparse buckets. `_prism_rt_bar_len` divides by `rt_count`, guarding the
// `rt_count == 0` case explicitly (every bar is empty, never a divide-by-zero) and clamping the
// result to `PRISM_RT_BAR_MAX` in case a hostile/inconsistent caller passes a bucket count larger
// than the stated total (this file does not cross-validate `sum(rt_distribution) == rt_count` --
// nothing in the task spec requires that invariant, and enforcing an unstated one would reject
// otherwise-valid input the caller has a right to supply).
//
// ── WHY prism_rt_score_badge's TEXT IS THE LITERAL "{score}/{max}", NEVER A DECIMAL ─────────────
// The task's own two illustrative examples ("4.2/5.0" or "85/100") are NOT both literal outputs
// of the same formatting rule -- if they were, "85/100" would need to be "85.0/100.0", which is
// not what a compact integer score badge should ever show. The task's own explicit format rule,
// given separately and taking precedence over the illustrative prose, is `"{score}/{max}"` --
// plain integer concatenation, `str(rt_score) + "/" + str(rt_max_score)`. A caller that genuinely
// has a "4.2" value already has it as a tenths-scaled int exactly like `rt_average` above and
// formats it into a string themselves before calling; this function does not guess which of its
// two callers' conventions (plain int vs tenths) is in play, because there is no way to tell them
// apart from an `int` alone.
//
// ── WHY AN ABSENT OPTIONAL TAIL OMITS THE WRAPPER ENTIRELY, MATCHING prism_fb_alert/toast ───────
// `prism_rt_review`'s `rt_date` and `prism_rt_score_badge`'s `rt_label` both follow the exact
// precedent `prism_ui_feedback.prism_fb_alert`'s `detail`/`prism_fb_toast`'s `dismiss_label`
// already set: when the optional part is `None` (or present-but-blank -- "no date" and "date is
// the empty string" are different facts, same reasoning as that file's own header), the function
// returns the smaller shape directly (the bare `band`/`stack` of the mandatory parts) rather than
// wrapping a single mandatory child in an otherwise-empty extra layer. This keeps the KAT's
// depth/count arithmetic identical in shape to every other optional-tail component in this
// codebase instead of inventing a third convention.
//
// ── WHY prism_rt_review_list's GAP IS FIXED, NOT FLEXIBLE ────────────────────────────────────────
// Same reasoning as `prism_st_card`'s own fixed `prism_gap(some(8))` (prism_ui_stat.nova): a
// flexible gap absorbs free space in a container that HAS free space to distribute, which is the
// wrong tool for a small, constant visual separator between two stacked review cards. Reused the
// identical `8`-unit value for the identical reason (an unremarkable, un-special breathing-room
// constant), named `PRISM_RT_LIST_GAP` here rather than shared as an import, matching every prior
// file's choice to keep its own small layout constants file-local rather than centralizing them.
//
// ── FIELD-SLOT COLLISION: N/A -- THIS FILE DECLARES NO STRUCTS ───────────────────────────────────
// Every value this file produces is either a plain scalar (string/int) or a `PrismNode` built
// through `prism_content`/`prism_arrange`'s own already-safe constructors; there is no new `type`
// or `enum` here, so the "prefix every field" discipline (reference_field_slot_collision_scales_
// with_imports.md) has nothing to apply to. If a future revision adds a struct (e.g. a typed
// review-item wrapper), every one of its fields MUST be `rt_`-prefixed before it ships.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   prism_rt_stars(rt_label: string, rt_value: int, rt_max: int) -> Result<PrismNode>
//     -> band[label, flow(icons)]; icons: first rt_value are check(), rest are minus(). Rejects
//     an empty rt_label, rt_max < 1, rt_value < 0, rt_value > rt_max.
//   prism_rt_review(rt_author: string, rt_rating: int, rt_max_rating: int, rt_body: string,
//                    rt_date: string?) -> Result<PrismNode>
//     -> stack[band[label(author), flow(stars)], label(body), label(date)?]; rt_date omitted or
//     blank drops the 3rd child entirely (see header). Rejects an empty rt_author, an empty
//     rt_body, and an invalid rating (same 3 rules as prism_rt_stars).
//   prism_rt_summary(rt_label: string, rt_average: int, rt_count: int, rt_distribution: list)
//                    -> Result<PrismNode>
//     -> stack[label(title), label("X.Y average from N reviews"), stack[5 distribution rows]];
//     rt_average is TENTHS (see header); rows run 5-star down to 1-star, each
//     band[label("L stars"), label(count), band(bar icons proportional to rt_count)]. Rejects an
//     empty rt_label, rt_average < 0, rt_count < 0, a distribution not exactly 5 elements, and any
//     element that is not a non-negative int (named by index).
//   prism_rt_review_list(reviews: list) -> Result<PrismNode>
//     -> stack[review, gap(8), review, gap(8), review, ...]; `reviews` is a bare list (runtime-
//     checked PrismNode elements, same discipline `prism_st_card`/`prism_tl_timeline` already
//     use). Rejects an empty list and a non-PrismNode element (named by index and actual type).
//   prism_rt_score_badge(rt_score: int, rt_max_score: int, rt_label: string?) -> Result<PrismNode>
//     -> band[label("{score}/{max}"), label(rt_label)?], or the bare score label alone when
//     rt_label is omitted or blank (see header). Rejects rt_max_score < 1, rt_score < 0,
//     rt_score > rt_max_score.

import prism_arrange
import prism_content
import prism_ui_kit

// ── Shared: "a value in [0, max], max itself >= 1" -- the EXACT rating-validity rule
// prism_rt_stars, prism_rt_review, and prism_rt_score_badge all need, each under different
// parameter names. Checked in the order a caller would want to read the failure: max's own
// validity first (checking a value against a bad max is meaningless), then the value's lower
// bound, then the value against the max -- the identical ordering discipline
// `prism_fb_progress`'s own total/done checks already use (prism_ui_feedback.nova).
fn _prism_rt_check_bounds(fn_name: string, value_name: string, value: int, max_name: string, max_val: int) -> Result<int>
    if max_val < 1
        return err(fn_name + ": " + max_name + " must be >= 1, got " + str(max_val))
    if value < 0
        return err(fn_name + ": " + value_name + " must be >= 0, got " + str(value))
    if value > max_val
        return err(fn_name + ": " + value_name + " (" + str(value) + ") must be <= " + max_name + " (" + str(max_val) + ")")
    ok(0)

// ── Shared: the check/minus star row itself, as a bare `flow` (no label) ────────────────────────
// PRIVATE and unvalidated on purpose: both call sites (`prism_rt_stars`, `prism_rt_review`) run
// `_prism_rt_check_bounds` first, so by the time this runs `0 <= value <= max_val` is already
// guaranteed -- re-checking it here would be dead code, the same "validate once, at the public
// boundary" discipline `_prism_in_rating_star` (prism_ui_input.nova) already follows for its own,
// differently-shaped, star loop.
fn _prism_rt_star_flow(value: int, max_val: int) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let icons = []
    for i in 0..max_val
        if i < value
            let icon = prism_content.prism_glyph(prism_content.prism_icon_check())?
            icons.push(icon)
        else
            let icon = prism_content.prism_glyph(prism_content.prism_icon_minus())?
            icons.push(icon)
    prism_arrange.prism_flow(icons)

// ── Star rating readout: label inline beside the star flow (see header for why `flow`, and why
// this is not prism_in_rating's shape) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn prism_rt_stars(rt_label: string, rt_value: int, rt_max: int) -> Result<PrismNode>
    if len(rt_label) == 0
        return err("prism_rt_stars: rt_label must not be empty")
    _prism_rt_check_bounds("prism_rt_stars", "rt_value", rt_value, "rt_max", rt_max)?
    let label_node = prism_content.prism_label(rt_label)?
    let flow_node = _prism_rt_star_flow(rt_value, rt_max)?
    prism_arrange.prism_band([label_node, flow_node])

// ── Review card: author + stars on top, body below, optional date last ──────────────────────────
// See header for why an absent/blank rt_date omits the 3rd child entirely rather than a stray
// blank line -- the same tolerance `prism_tl_item`'s optional description already applies.
fn prism_rt_review(rt_author: string, rt_rating: int, rt_max_rating: int, rt_body: string, rt_date: string?) -> Result<PrismNode>
    if len(rt_author) == 0
        return err("prism_rt_review: rt_author must not be empty")
    if len(rt_body) == 0
        return err("prism_rt_review: rt_body must not be empty")
    _prism_rt_check_bounds("prism_rt_review", "rt_rating", rt_rating, "rt_max_rating", rt_max_rating)?
    let author_node = prism_content.prism_label(rt_author)?
    let stars_node = _prism_rt_star_flow(rt_rating, rt_max_rating)?
    let head = prism_arrange.prism_band([author_node, stars_node])?
    let body_node = prism_content.prism_label(rt_body)?
    match rt_date
        None => prism_arrange.prism_stack([head, body_node])
        Some(d) =>
            if len(d) == 0
                return prism_arrange.prism_stack([head, body_node])
            let date_node = prism_content.prism_label(d)?
            prism_arrange.prism_stack([head, body_node, date_node])

// ── Rating summary: title + "X.Y average from N reviews" + a 5-row distribution breakdown ───────
// A full bar's worth of icons for a bucket that IS the entire total -- see header for why this is
// scaled against rt_count, not the largest bucket, and why the result is clamped defensively.
let PRISM_RT_BAR_MAX = 10

fn _prism_rt_bar_len(count: int, total: int) -> int
    if total <= 0
        return 0
    min((count * PRISM_RT_BAR_MAX) / total, PRISM_RT_BAR_MAX)

fn _prism_rt_bar(count: int, total: int) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let bar_len = _prism_rt_bar_len(count, total)
    let icons = []
    for i in 0..bar_len
        let icon = prism_content.prism_glyph(prism_content.prism_icon_check())?
        icons.push(icon)
    prism_arrange.prism_band(icons)

// One distribution row: "L stars" + the raw count + the proportional bar, in that order.
fn _prism_rt_dist_row(level: int, count: int, total: int) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let star_label = prism_content.prism_label(str(level) + " stars")?
    let count_label = prism_content.prism_label(str(count))?
    let bar_node = _prism_rt_bar(count, total)?
    prism_arrange.prism_band([star_label, count_label, bar_node])

// `rt_distribution` is a bare `list` of ints (not `list<int>` -- mirroring every other bare-list
// parameter in this codebase's ui/ layer), index 0 = 1-star ... index 4 = 5-star (the task's own
// mapping). Rows render 5-star first, 1-star last -- the familiar breakdown order every real
// rating-summary UI uses (Amazon, app stores), computed by walking `i` upward (0..5) and reading
// `idx = 4 - i` so no descending-range syntax is needed.
fn prism_rt_summary(rt_label: string, rt_average: int, rt_count: int, rt_distribution: list) -> Result<PrismNode>
    if len(rt_label) == 0
        return err("prism_rt_summary: rt_label must not be empty")
    if rt_average < 0
        return err("prism_rt_summary: rt_average must be >= 0, got " + str(rt_average))
    if rt_count < 0
        return err("prism_rt_summary: rt_count must be >= 0, got " + str(rt_count))
    if len(rt_distribution) != 5
        return err("prism_rt_summary: rt_distribution must have exactly 5 elements, got " + str(len(rt_distribution)))
    for idx, c in rt_distribution
        if not is_int(c)
            return err("prism_rt_summary: rt_distribution[" + str(idx) + "] must be an int, got " + type_name(c))
        if c < 0
            return err("prism_rt_summary: rt_distribution[" + str(idx) + "] must be >= 0, got " + str(c))
    let title_node = prism_content.prism_label(rt_label)?
    let avg_whole = rt_average / 10
    let avg_frac = rt_average % 10
    let avg_text = str(avg_whole) + "." + str(avg_frac) + " average from " + str(rt_count) + " reviews"
    let avg_node = prism_content.prism_label(avg_text)?
    let rows = []
    for i in 0..5
        let idx = 4 - i
        let level = idx + 1
        let row = _prism_rt_dist_row(level, rt_distribution[idx], rt_count)?
        rows.push(row)
    let dist_block = prism_arrange.prism_stack(rows)?
    prism_arrange.prism_stack([title_node, avg_node, dist_block])

// ── Review list: reviews stacked with a fixed gap between each pair (see header for why fixed) ──
let PRISM_RT_LIST_GAP = 8

// `reviews` is a bare `list` (not `list<PrismNode>` -- same self-referential-generic caution
// prism_node.nova's own header documents), so every element is guarded with the same
// `type_name(...) != "PrismNode"` check `prism_st_card`/`prism_tl_timeline` already use, naming
// the offending index and actual type on rejection.
fn prism_rt_review_list(reviews: list) -> Result<PrismNode>
    if len(reviews) == 0
        return err("prism_rt_review_list: reviews must not be empty")
    for idx, r in reviews
        if type_name(r) != "PrismNode"
            return err("prism_rt_review_list: review " + str(idx) + " is not a PrismNode (got " + type_name(r) + ")")
    let out = []
    for idx, r in reviews
        if idx > 0
            let gap_node = prism_arrange.prism_gap(some(PRISM_RT_LIST_GAP))?
            out.push(gap_node)
        out.push(r)
    prism_arrange.prism_stack(out)

// ── Score badge: a compact "{score}/{max}" with an optional trailing label ──────────────────────
// See header for why the text is the literal integer format, never a synthesized decimal, and why
// an absent/blank rt_label returns the bare score label rather than a 1-child band.
fn prism_rt_score_badge(rt_score: int, rt_max_score: int, rt_label: string?) -> Result<PrismNode>
    _prism_rt_check_bounds("prism_rt_score_badge", "rt_score", rt_score, "rt_max_score", rt_max_score)?
    let score_text = str(rt_score) + "/" + str(rt_max_score)
    let score_node = prism_content.prism_label(score_text)?
    match rt_label
        None => ok(score_node)
        Some(text) =>
            if len(text) == 0
                return ok(score_node)
            let label_node = prism_content.prism_label(text)?
            prism_arrange.prism_band([score_node, label_node])
